WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.560
Announcer: Ladies and gentlemen, the Vice President
of the United States and Mrs. Cheney.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:40.640
Announcer: Ladies and gentlemen, Christopher Demuth,
President of the American Enterprise Institute,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.400
and Charles Krauthammer, 2004 Irving Kristol
Award recipient.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.520
Chris DeMuth: Ladies and gentlemen, we will
begin by singing America the "My Country,

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.620
'Tis of Thee, God Save the Queen" version.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:33.880
I know that everyone present knows the word
by heart, but for those who would like a refresher,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.430
the words are printed in the middle of your
program.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:45.120
We will be led by my AEI colleagues, Alexandra
Obraskan [SP], Osea Dosova, and Heather Dresser,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.160
please.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:09.520
My country, 'tis of thee,
Sweet land of liberty,

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:21.680
Of thee I sing;
Land where my fathers died,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:32.530
Land of the pilgrims' pride,
From ev'ry mountainside

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.730
Let freedom ring!

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:52.180
Our fathers' God to Thee,
Author of liberty,

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.400
To Thee we sing.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:06.170
Long may our land be bright,
With freedom's holy light,

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:27.380
Protect us by Thy might,
Great God our King.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.100
Chris DeMuth: Thank you very much.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.350
Ladies and gentlemen, please be seated.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.800
Vice President and Mrs. Cheney, colleagues
and distinguished guests, welcome to the annual

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:51.300
dinner of the American Enterprise Institute
for Public Policy Research.This is our largest

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:57.550
annual gathering since Ronald Reagan gave
his final major address in Washington in December

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.120
of 1988.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.069
I'm deeply grateful to our many, generous
sponsors, especially our friends, advisor

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:09.330
and members of our dinner committee.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:17.829
George Shultz was once asked, "What is the
most important qualification for a senior

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:19.750
position in government?"

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:27.080
He replied, "A high tolerance for ambiguity."

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:32.020
Presidents and Cabinet Secretaries only get
to make hard calls, the easy calls are made

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.000
by others.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.620
Many decisions have good arguments on both
sides, but the really hard ones are those

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.830
that combine very large stakes with very large
uncertainties.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:50.469
They call for intelligence of the larger and
more profound kind, and a determination not

00:04:50.469 --> 00:04:53.789
to lose a kingdom for a shoe.

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:59.210
If we were to form a commission of inquiry
into every important Oval Office decision

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:03.710
made without the luxury of waiting for more
and better information, there would be no

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:10.050
more worries about job creation in the service
sector.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.869
And in a fractious democracy such as ours,
the hard decisions are endlessly contested,

00:05:14.869 --> 00:05:20.360
in public debate, in competition among the
three branches of government, and even within

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.870
the Executive Branch itself.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:28.830
For us, the biggest decisions are not executive
calls at all, but rather a process of continuous

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:33.499
advocacy, compromise, and adaptation.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:38.330
During the past 29 months, America and the
Free World have entered a new political epoch,

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:45.129
whose defining features are, precisely, terribly
large risks and terribly large uncertainties,

00:05:45.129 --> 00:05:48.850
especially concerning the operations of our
enemies.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:54.410
Our political leaders have made a series of
momentous decisions and have done so with

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:55.749
great resolution.

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:01.009
Their resolution is all the more impressive
when one considers that the deficiencies of

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:06.599
information, and of government institutions
designed for an earlier era, are better known

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.419
to them than to anyone else.

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:14.020
Now their decisions and their resolution are
being tested in the cauldron of democratic

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.169
politics.

00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:21.810
Tolerance for ambiguity is not a predominant
feature of partisan campaigning.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:27.460
But elections themselves have clarified and
fortified our foreign policies to an impressive

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.409
degree in the past.

00:06:30.409 --> 00:06:35.460
In the Cold War, from the Truman Doctrine
to the Reagan Doctrine, our enemies were demonstrating

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:42.020
their WMDs out in the open, bragging about
how they would use them against us, routinely

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:47.900
invading and subjugating other nations, and
slaughtering civilians by the millions.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:54.659
Still, fashioning and sustaining a political
consensus was very hard, and contested from

00:06:54.659 --> 00:06:56.629
start to finish.

00:06:56.629 --> 00:07:02.479
Many Americans sincerely believed, and argued
vehemently, that the root of the world's problems

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:04.789
was America itself.

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:06.270
But we prevailed.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:12.099
And we prevailed not in spite of, but because
of the vigor of our democratic practices and

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:15.699
the sturdiness of popular understanding.

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:20.929
Can we do it again, in circumstances more
shrouded and insidious?

00:07:20.929 --> 00:07:27.050
Today, despite stupendous initial victories,
we are in the midst of strenuous and necessary

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:32.520
debates over military and political strategy,
the effectiveness of our intelligence and

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:38.949
diplomatic institutions, and the maintenance
of fiscal discipline and personal and economic

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:41.439
freedom at home.

00:07:41.439 --> 00:07:46.429
But if we stand at the beginning of another
50-year epoch, a "generational commitment"

00:07:46.429 --> 00:07:52.229
as Condoleezza Rice puts it, then we are still
just getting started, still at the moment

00:07:52.229 --> 00:08:00.779
of creation of new policies and new institutions
for facing the tasks and perils ahead.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:05.469
That the two men who are speaking to us this
evening have been present at the creation

00:08:05.469 --> 00:08:09.069
is another great stroke of American fortune.

00:08:09.069 --> 00:08:17.099
One, the recipient of AEI's Irving Kristol
Award for 2004, is a man of thinking and writing.

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:23.030
The other, a former AEI senior fellow and
vice chairman of our Board, now Vice President

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:28.210
of the United States, is a man of thinking
and action.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:34.320
Charles Krauthammer, his great contribution
has been to see through fogs of uncertainty

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.380
in times of upheaval.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:42.780
He has grasped the essentials of new problems
while others remained confused by incidentals.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:47.880
More than once, he has provided us with an
intellectual architecture that shows that

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:53.390
what we already know provides a sturdy foundation
for action.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.240
Charles is also an avid amateur chess player.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.960
He is a founding member, along with AEI's
Charles Murray, of one of Washington's most

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:08.300
exclusive secret societies, the Pariah Chess
Club.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:13.690
His Irving Kristol Award consists of this
exquisite John Jack's Stan and Chess set and

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.310
hand-crafted board.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:22.720
Our media, which can take the silliest people
and ideas seriously, often find truly serious

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.020
men and women a bafflement.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:31.360
It is very funny to read the latest line on
Dick Cheney, that this open and engaging man,

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.280
who has expressed himself on every manner
of policy question in 30 years at the center

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:41.540
of American politics, who has been Minority
Whip of the House of Representatives, who

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:47.110
has served in the senior ranks of four administrations,
who has become the activist Vice President

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:53.250
par excellence, is just now emerging from
obscurity, getting around town, attending

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:58.580
coming-out parties, and plunging into the
thick of public debate.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:04.510
At AEI, we admire people who think before
they speak, who choose their words carefully,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:10.970
and who understand when the time for research
is over and the time for decision has arrived.

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:15.260
We are very honored that the vice president
would continue to attend our councils while

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:30.140
on extended leave-of-absence through 2009
or 2017.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.780
Tonight he has graciously agreed to introduce
our Irving Kristol Lecturer.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:41.260
Ladies and gentlemen, the Vice President of
the United States.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.480
Vice President Cheney: Thank you.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.420
Thank you all very much.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.180
Thank you, Chris.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:19.271
And it's a pleasure tonight to join all of
you in honoring Charles Krauthammer, a man

00:11:19.271 --> 00:11:23.750
I admire very much, and am proud to call a
friend.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:28.990
The Irving Kristol Award is named for one
great American, and tonight we bestow it on

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:30.970
another.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:36.570
Lynne and I are pleased, as well, to be in
the company of so many other friends and colleagues,

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:46.690
starting with Chris DeMuth, who does an absolutely
superb job as President of AEI.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:58.740
Being here brings to mind my own days affiliated
with AEI, which stretch back some 30 years,

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:08.810
as an office holder, a freshman congressman,
an out-of-work politician, a member of the

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:14.290
Board of Trustees, and a corporate official,
who didn't appreciate how valuable the experience

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:20.650
was till I was asked to contribute financially
for the privilege of being part of it.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:26.170
But it has been a very, very important part
of our lives, for me and for Lynne, and a

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:33.690
very important part of our intellectual learning
and development during our years in Washington.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:43.970
I spent a time at AEI when I was a scholar,
a time when I had an office, a small staff,

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.660
and not much in the way of actual responsibility.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:54.560
It turned out to be a lot like the vice presidency.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:58.420
Lynne and I are truly grateful for our many
years of association with the American Enterprise

00:12:58.420 --> 00:12:59.420
Institute.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:06.590
AEI has developed a reputation, well-deserved,
for disciplined scholarship, intellectual

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:10.690
integrity, and fresh insight into public policy.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:16.660
And AEI continues to earn that reputation
every year with research and writing of high

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.810
standards and ever-increasing influence.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:27.100
Few at AEI are more influential than the chairman
of our Board of Academic Advisers, Professor

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:33.060
James Q. Wilson, who, last July, received
the Medal of Freedom from President Bush.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:39.470
I've known Jim for a number of years, I've
respected his work ever since I was a graduate

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:44.290
student, in the days when Lynne and I were
both working on our PhDs.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:49.210
Lynne actually went on to earn her PhD in
British literature.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:56.980
I haven't quite settled on a topic for my
dissertation yet.

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:02.890
For me, an expected career in academic life
was overtaken by a series of opportunities

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:04.500
in government.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:09.040
And so, I have spent much of the last three-and-a-half
decades in and around this city.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:15.860
Here, where our national debates are centered,
you get used to the shifting attention and

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:22.780
the passing enthusiasms that characterize
so much of our political commentary.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:29.029
You learn to take it all in, and then to select
out the well-considered judgments of a serious

00:14:29.029 --> 00:14:30.670
thinker.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:35.720
You begin to listen through the chorus in
search of that one clear note.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:41.970
And so often, that clear note is the commentary
of Charles Krauthammer.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:47.490
This most respected of writers is also a distinguished
medical doctor who spent years in practice

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:49.940
as a noted psychiatrist.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:54.910
He first came to Washington in the 1970s,
and soon found himself working at the White

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:58.100
House for one of my predecessors.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:06.951
I now wish I had paid more attention at the
time to the speeches of Walter Mondale, because

00:15:06.951 --> 00:15:13.110
I'm sure they were absolutely first rate.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:19.430
By the early 1980s, Charles's talent had been
recognized by editors and by readers in Washington

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:20.590
and well beyond.

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:27.800
And the most impressive aspect of his work
is the sustained level of quality over a period

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:30.040
of more than 20 years.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:35.730
This is not a columnist who merely fills space
and meets deadlines.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:39.470
Charles Krauthammer always writes with care.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:44.950
In his columns and essays, there is always
a powerful line of reasoning, and behind it

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:48.430
the workings of a superior intellect.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:53.660
When you read his words, you know you are
dealing with a serious person, who assumes

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:55.850
the same of you.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:59.440
You see something else, as well, in a Krauthammer
column.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:05.089
Whatever the subject at hand, Charles gives
the reader evidence and argument, never just

00:16:05.089 --> 00:16:08.920
sentiment and the conventional wisdom.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:15.600
His great intelligence is guided by principle
and an understanding of the world as it is.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:20.640
These qualities produce special insights into
the very areas where we need them most, from

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:27.089
the new powers mankind has assumed in science,
to the new dangers confronting America and

00:16:27.089 --> 00:16:28.779
other free nations.

00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:36.070
A consistent theme in Charles's writings is
his belief in human freedom, and his abhorrence

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:40.190
for violence and tyranny.

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:45.300
Since September 11th, Charles has written
compellingly on the urgent duty of free nations

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:52.860
to defeat the terrorists, and hold to account
any regime that supports or arms them.

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:58.880
This war on terror has in many ways brought
out the finest qualities of the American people.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:04.839
And the complexities of this era have certainly
brought out the finest attributes of this

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:13.129
writer, his wisdom, his deep moral sensibility,
and his conviction that freedom is the right

00:17:13.129 --> 00:17:17.980
of all mankind and must be defended.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:23.119
The citation for the Irving Kristol Award
for 2004 reads as follows:

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:25.160
To Charles Krauthammer:

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:34.080
Fearless journalist, wise analyst, and militant
democrat, who has shown that America's interests

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:40.259
and ideals are indivisible, and that the promotion
of freedom is hard-headed realism.

00:17:40.259 --> 00:17:46.600
I'm very pleased that Charles's wife, Robyn,
and their son, Daniel, are here to witness

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:52.620
the presentation of this award, and to see
the respect and affection we all feel for

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:53.620
its recipient.

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:59.840
It is my privilege to introduce the great
man we honor tonight, Dr. Charles Krauthammer.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:23.960
Dr. Krauthammer: Thank you.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.880
Thank you, Mr. Vice President.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.000
Thank you, Mr. Vice President.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:34.440
Thank you, Mr. Vice President.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:38.200
Thank you, Mr. Vice President.

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:40.920
Thank you for those very kind words.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:52.840
I'm honored by your presence here, especially
during duck hunting season.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:59.980
And as a citizen, I wanna thank you not only
for your leadership and your wisdom during

00:18:59.980 --> 00:19:05.309
these extraordinary times, but for your courage.

00:19:05.309 --> 00:19:10.690
If Hamlet had borne half the slings and arrows
you have, it would have been a very short

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:14.159
play.

00:19:14.159 --> 00:19:21.330
Seeing my checker past recalled in the program,
I'm struck by how many places I have fled,

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:34.480
the democratic party, Canada and psychiatry,
the trifecta.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:42.470
The reason I'm here ladies and gentlemen,
is that I have nowhere left to go.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:50.450
I wanna thank Chris DeMuth, Jim Wilson and
the AEI Counsel for thinking otherwise and

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:57.740
for bestowing upon me this great honor, particularly
one that carries the name of my dear and revered

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:02.220
friend, Irving Kristol.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:07.150
My subject tonight, is "American Foreign Policy."

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:10.549
Americans have healthy aversion to foreign
policy.

00:20:10.549 --> 00:20:13.500
It stems from a sense of thrift.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:15.879
Who needs it?

00:20:15.879 --> 00:20:18.629
We're protected by two great oceans.

00:20:18.629 --> 00:20:21.960
We have this continent practically to ourselves.

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:27.769
And we share it with only two neighbors, both
friendly, one so friendly that its people

00:20:27.769 --> 00:20:35.080
seem intent upon moving in with us.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:40.950
It took three giants of the 20th Century to
drag us into its great battles, Wilson into

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:46.499
World War I, Roosevelt into World War II,
Truman into the Cold War.

00:20:46.499 --> 00:20:52.149
And then it ended with one of the great anticlimaxes
in history.

00:20:52.149 --> 00:20:56.990
Without a shot fired, without a revolution,
without so much as a press release, the Soviet

00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:01.429
Union simply gave up and disappeared.

00:21:01.429 --> 00:21:07.419
It was the end of everything, the end of communism,
of socialism, of the Cold War, of the European

00:21:07.419 --> 00:21:08.489
wars.

00:21:08.489 --> 00:21:14.059
It was the end of e Russian Empire, an empire
was grooved by swallowing the equivalent of

00:21:14.059 --> 00:21:19.419
a Belgium every year for 200 years.

00:21:19.419 --> 00:21:34.159
Given how Brussels has behaved recently, overall,
not a bud idea.

00:21:34.159 --> 00:21:37.179
But the end of everything was also a beginning.

00:21:37.179 --> 00:21:43.820
On December 26, 1991, the Soviet Union died
and something new was born, something utterly

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:50.429
new, a unipolar world dominated by a single
superpower unchecked by any rival and with

00:21:50.429 --> 00:21:58.410
decisive reach in every corner of the globe.This
is a staggering new development in human history,

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.659
not seen since the fall of Rome.

00:22:00.659 --> 00:22:06.340
It is so new, so strange, that we have no
idea how to deal with it.

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:11.619
Our first reaction, the 1990s, was utter confusion.

00:22:11.619 --> 00:22:14.460
The next reaction was awe.

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:20.610
When Paul Kennedy, who had once popularized
the idea of American decline, saw what America

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:27.950
did in the Afghan war, a display of fully
mobilized, furiously concentrated unipolar

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:31.260
power at a distance of 8,000 miles.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:39.950
He not only recanted, he stood in wonder,
"Nothing has ever existed like this disparity

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:43.200
of power," he wrote, "Nothing.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:45.460
No other nation comes close.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:49.139
Charlemagne's empire was merely western European
in its reach.

00:22:49.139 --> 00:22:54.889
The Roman empire stretched farther afield,
but there was another great empire in Persia,

00:22:54.889 --> 00:22:56.759
and a larger one in China.

00:22:56.759 --> 00:23:00.950
There is, therefore, no comparison."

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:04.110
Even Rome is no model for what America is
today.

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:08.249
First, because we do not have the imperial
culture of Rome.

00:23:08.249 --> 00:23:15.899
We are an Athenian republic, even more republican
and infinitely more democratic than Athens.

00:23:15.899 --> 00:23:20.460
And this American Republic has acquired the
largest seeming empire in the history of the

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:26.100
world, acquired it in a fit of absent-mindedness
greater even than Britain's.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:30.570
And it was not just absent-mindedness, it
was sheer inadvertence.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:36.279
We got here because of Europe's suicide in
the world wars of the 20th century, and then

00:23:36.279 --> 00:23:41.899
the death of its Eurasian successor, Soviet
Russia, for having adopted a political and

00:23:41.899 --> 00:23:48.239
economic system so inhuman that, like a genetically
defective organism, it simply expired in its

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:52.779
sleep, leaving us with global dominion.

00:23:52.779 --> 00:23:59.789
Second, we are unlike Rome, unlike Britain
and France and Spain and the other classical

00:23:59.789 --> 00:24:06.809
empires of modern times, in that we do not
hunger for territory.

00:24:06.809 --> 00:24:10.330
The use of the word "empire" in the American
context is ridiculous.

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:17.330
It is absurd to apply the word to a people
whose first instincts upon arriving on anyone's

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:26.110
soil is to demand an exit strategy.

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:36.619
I can assure you that when the Romans went
into Gaul and the British into India, they

00:24:36.619 --> 00:24:39.419
were not looking for exit strategies.

00:24:39.419 --> 00:24:43.519
They were looking for entry strategies.

00:24:43.519 --> 00:24:49.899
In David Lean's Lawrence of Arabia, King Faisal
says to Lawrence, "I think you are another

00:24:49.899 --> 00:24:51.321
of these desert-loving English.

00:24:51.321 --> 00:24:56.059
The English have a great hunger for desolate
places."

00:24:56.059 --> 00:25:02.399
Indeed, for five centuries, the Europeans
did hunger for deserts and jungles and oceans

00:25:02.399 --> 00:25:04.780
and new continents.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:06.590
Americans do not.

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:09.059
We like it here.

00:25:09.059 --> 00:25:10.249
We like our McDonald's.

00:25:10.249 --> 00:25:11.470
We like our football.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:12.669
We like our rock-and-roll.

00:25:12.669 --> 00:25:24.330
And until 10 days ago, we liked our half-time
shows.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:26.460
We've got the Grand Canyon and Graceland.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:28.989
We've got Silicon Valley and South Beach.

00:25:28.989 --> 00:25:31.020
We've got everything.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:37.879
And if that's not enough, we've got the Vegas,
which is a facsimile of everything.

00:25:37.879 --> 00:25:41.000
What could we possibly need anywhere else?

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:42.879
We don't like exotic climates.

00:25:42.879 --> 00:25:45.200
We don't like exotic languages.

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:49.860
All those declensions and moods, we don't
even know what a mood is.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:55.200
We like Iowa corn and New York hot dogs, and
if we want Chinese, or Indian, or Italian,

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.279
we go to the food court.

00:25:56.279 --> 00:26:01.669
We don't send the Marines for takeout.

00:26:01.669 --> 00:26:04.169
That's because we are not an imperial power.

00:26:04.169 --> 00:26:06.549
We are a commercial republic.

00:26:06.549 --> 00:26:12.879
We don't take food, we trade for it, which
makes us something unique in history, an anomaly,

00:26:12.879 --> 00:26:17.720
a hybrid, a commercial republic with overwhelming
global power.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:23.570
A commercial republic that, by pure accident
of history, has been designated custodian

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:26.109
of the international system.

00:26:26.109 --> 00:26:33.749
The eyes of every supplicant from East Timor
to Afghanistan, from Iraq to Liberia, Arab

00:26:33.749 --> 00:26:39.039
and Israeli, Irish and British, North and
South Korean are upon us.

00:26:39.039 --> 00:26:40.210
That is who we are.

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.840
That is where we are.

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:44.999
And now the question is: What do we do?

00:26:44.999 --> 00:26:49.270
What is a unipolar power to do?

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:55.460
The oldest and most venerable answer is to
hoard that power and retreat.

00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:57.960
This is known as isolationism.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:03.619
Of all the foreign policy schools in America,
it has the oldest pedigree, not surprising

00:27:03.619 --> 00:27:09.440
in the country protected by two great oceans.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:14.119
Isolationism originally sprang from a view
of America as spiritually superior to the

00:27:14.119 --> 00:27:15.809
old world.

00:27:15.809 --> 00:27:22.000
We were too good to be corrupted by its low
intrigues, entangled by its cynical alliances.

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:27.019
Today, however, isolationism is an ideology
of fear.

00:27:27.019 --> 00:27:28.389
Fear of trade.

00:27:28.389 --> 00:27:29.389
Fear of immigrants.

00:27:29.389 --> 00:27:31.769
Fear of the Other.

00:27:31.769 --> 00:27:36.019
Isolationists want to cut off trade and immigration,
and withdraw from our military and strategic

00:27:36.019 --> 00:27:38.490
commitments around the world.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:43.370
Even isolationists, of course, did not oppose
the war in Afghanistan, because it was so

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:50.820
obviously an act of self-defense, only a fool
or a knave or a Susan Sontag could oppose

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:56.549
that.

00:27:56.549 --> 00:28:00.669
But anything beyond that, isolationists oppose.

00:28:00.669 --> 00:28:06.999
They are for a radical retrenchment of American
power for pulling up the drawbridge to Fortress

00:28:06.999 --> 00:28:09.239
America.

00:28:09.239 --> 00:28:13.559
Isolationism is an important school of thought
historically, but not today.

00:28:13.559 --> 00:28:19.039
Not just because of its brutal intellectual
reductionism, but because it is so obviously

00:28:19.039 --> 00:28:25.179
inappropriate to the world of today, a world
of export-driven economies, of massive population

00:28:25.179 --> 00:28:32.380
flows, and of 9/11, the definitive demonstration
that the combination of modern technology

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:41.950
and transnational primitivism has erased the
barrier between "over there" and "over here."

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:48.869
Classical isolationism is not just intellectually
obsolete, it is politically bankrupt as well.

00:28:48.869 --> 00:28:54.440
Four years ago, its most public advocate,
Pat Buchanan, ran for president of the United

00:28:54.440 --> 00:29:10.149
States, and carried Palm Beach, by accident.

00:29:10.149 --> 00:29:12.570
Classic isolationism is moribund.

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:15.850
Who then rules America?

00:29:15.850 --> 00:29:19.700
In the 1990s, it was liberal internationalism.

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:24.299
Liberal internationalism is the foreign policy
of the Democratic Party and the religion of

00:29:24.299 --> 00:29:26.619
the foreign policy elite.

00:29:26.619 --> 00:29:28.330
It has a peculiar history.

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:35.100
It traces its pedigree to Woodrow Wilson's
utopianism, Harry Truman's anticommunism,

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.350
and John Kennedy's militant universalism.

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:43.980
But after the Vietnam War, it was transmuted
into an ideology of passivity, acquiescence

00:29:43.980 --> 00:29:48.080
and almost reflexive anti-interventionism.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:54.549
Liberals today proudly take credit for Truman's
and Kennedy's role in containing communism,

00:29:54.549 --> 00:29:59.989
but what they prefer to forget is that, for
the last half of the Cold War, liberals used

00:29:59.989 --> 00:30:03.409
"cold warrior" as an epithet.

00:30:03.409 --> 00:30:08.239
In the early 1980s, they gave us the freeze
movement, a form of unilateral disarmament

00:30:08.239 --> 00:30:11.000
in the face of Soviet nuclear advances.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:18.480
Today, John Kerry boasts of opposing, during
the 1980s, what he calls Ronald Reagan's "illegal

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.640
war in Central America."

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:27.610
And oppose he did what was, in fact, an indigenous
anticommunist rebellion that ultimately succeeded

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:43.779
in bringing down Sandinista rule and ushering
in democracy in all of Central America.

00:30:43.779 --> 00:30:51.700
That boast reminds us how militant was liberal
passivity in the last half of the Cold War.

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:54.740
But that passivity outlived the Cold War.

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:59.440
When Kuwait was invaded and the question was,
"Should the United States go to war to prevent

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:03.950
the Persian Gulf from falling into hostile
hands?"

00:31:03.950 --> 00:31:09.769
The Democratic Party joined the Buchananite
isolationists in saying no.

00:31:09.769 --> 00:31:16.049
The Democrats voted No overwhelmingly, two
to one in the House, more than four to one

00:31:16.049 --> 00:31:18.119
in the Senate.

00:31:18.119 --> 00:31:24.570
And yet, quite astonishingly, when liberal
internationalism came to power just two years

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:29.859
later in the form of the Clinton administration,
it turned almost hyper-interventionist.

00:31:29.859 --> 00:31:37.210
It involved us four times in military action:
deepening intervention in Somalia, invading

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:44.330
Haiti, bombing Bosnia, and finally going to
war over Kosovo.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:51.570
How do you explain the amazing transmutation
of Cold War and Gulf War doves into Haiti

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:53.320
and Balkan hawks?

00:31:53.320 --> 00:32:00.210
The crucial and obvious difference is this,
Haiti, Bosnia and Kosovo were humanitarian

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:05.379
ventures, fights for right and good, devoid
of raw national interest.

00:32:05.379 --> 00:32:11.159
And not only humanitarian intervention, disinterested
interventionism devoid of national interest

00:32:11.159 --> 00:32:15.710
is morally pristine enough to justify the
use of force.

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:20.239
The history of the '90s refutes the lazy notion
that liberals have an aversion to the use

00:32:20.239 --> 00:32:21.239
of force.

00:32:21.239 --> 00:32:22.239
They do not.

00:32:22.239 --> 00:32:28.049
They have an aversion to using force for reasons
of pure national interest.

00:32:28.049 --> 00:32:32.320
And by national interest, I do not mean simple
self-defense.

00:32:32.320 --> 00:32:34.629
Everyone believes in self-defense, as in Afghanistan.

00:32:34.629 --> 00:32:40.149
I'm talking about national interest as defined
by a great power, shaping the international

00:32:40.149 --> 00:32:46.470
environment by projecting power abroad to
secure economic, political, and strategic

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:48.480
goods.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:54.190
Intervening militarily for that kind of national
interest, liberal internationalism finds unholy

00:32:54.190 --> 00:32:55.869
and unsupportable.

00:32:55.869 --> 00:33:02.109
It sees that kind of national interest as
merely self-interest writ large, in effect,

00:33:02.109 --> 00:33:04.950
a form of grand national selfishness.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.649
Hence Kuwait, no.

00:33:07.649 --> 00:33:09.570
Kosovo, yes.

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:14.279
The other defining feature of the Clinton
foreign policy was multilateralism, which

00:33:14.279 --> 00:33:17.659
expressed itself in a mania for treaties.

00:33:17.659 --> 00:33:24.509
The Clinton administration negotiated a dizzying
succession of parchment promises on bioweapons,

00:33:24.509 --> 00:33:31.489
chemical weapons, nuclear testing, carbon
emissions, antiballistic missiles, etc.

00:33:31.489 --> 00:33:33.109
Why?

00:33:33.109 --> 00:33:37.599
No sentient being could believe that, say,
the chemical or biological weapons treaties

00:33:37.599 --> 00:33:40.909
were anything more than transparently useless.

00:33:40.909 --> 00:33:46.729
Senator Joe Biden once defended the Chemical
Weapons Convention, which even its proponents

00:33:46.729 --> 00:33:51.649
admitted was unenforceable, on the grounds
that it would "provide us with a valuable

00:33:51.649 --> 00:33:58.349
tool", the "moral suasion of the entire international
community."

00:33:58.349 --> 00:34:00.169
Moral suasion?

00:34:00.169 --> 00:34:05.019
The whole point of this treaty was to keep
rogue states from developing chemical weapons.

00:34:05.019 --> 00:34:10.460
Rogue states are, by definition, impervious
to moral suasion.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:15.570
Was it moral suasion that made Qaddafi see
the wisdom of giving up his weapons of mass

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.730
destruction?

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:22.120
Or Iran agree for the first time to spot nuclear
inspections?

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:25.100
It was the suasion of the bayonet.

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:29.970
It was the ignominious fall of Saddam and
the desire of interested spectators not to

00:34:29.970 --> 00:34:43.110
be next on the list.

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.460
Moral suasion is a farce.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:51.740
Why then this obsession with protocols, legalisms
UN resolutions?

00:34:51.740 --> 00:34:57.920
Its obvious net effect and after all is to
temper American power and reduce American

00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:05.150
freedom of action by making it dependent on
constricted by serving into the will and interest

00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:07.440
of other nations.

00:35:07.440 --> 00:35:14.430
But that, you see, is the whole point of the
multilateral enterprise, to tie down Gulliver

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:21.820
with a thousand strings, to domesticate the
most undomesticated, most outsized, national

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.020
interest on the planet, ours.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:29.030
Who, after all, was really going to be most
constrained by these treaties?

00:35:29.030 --> 00:35:30.590
North Korea?

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:36.770
Today, multilateralism remains the overriding
theme of liberal internationalism.

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:43.250
When in power in the '90s, multilateralism
expressed itself as a mania for treaties.

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:49.990
When out of power in this decade, it manifests
itself in the slavish pursuit of "international

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:56.360
legitimacy," and opposition to any American
action undertaken without universal foreign

00:35:56.360 --> 00:36:01.640
blessing, which is why the Democratic critique
of the war in Iraq is so peculiarly one of

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:04.390
process and not of policy.

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:07.510
The problem was that we did not have the permission
of the UN.

00:36:07.510 --> 00:36:12.140
We did not have a large enough coalition;
we did not have a second Security Council

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:13.400
resolution.

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:17.640
Kofi Annan was unhappy and the French were
cross.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:21.780
The Democratic presidential candidates all
say we should have internationalized the conflict,

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:28.980
brought in the UN, enlisted the allies, for
two reasons, they say, assistance and legitimacy.

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:35.910
First, they say, we could have used these
other countries to help us in the reconstruction.

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:38.830
This is rich.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:41.140
Everyone would like to have more help in reconstruction.

00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:44.300
It would have been lovely to have the Germans
and the French in Baghdad.

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:57.930
So Germans the ca'do the policing, the French
ca'do the catering.

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:01.070
But the question is moot, and the argument
is cynical.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:06.130
France and Germany made it absolutely clear
that they would never support the overthrow

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:07.480
of Saddam.

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:12.670
So, accommodating them was not a way to get
them into the reconstruction, it was a way

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:19.720
to ensure that there would never be any reconstruction,
because Saddam would still be in power.

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:24.530
Of course, it would be nice if we had more
allies rather than less.

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:28.530
It would also be nice to be able to fly.

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:34.240
But when some nations are not with you on
your enterprise, including them in your coalition

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:39.170
is not a way to broaden it, it's a way to
abolish it.

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:45.360
At which point, liberal internationalists
switch gears and appeal to legitimacy?

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:54.250
On the grounds that multilateral action has
a higher moral standing.

00:37:54.250 --> 00:38:00.370
I have always found that line of argument
utterly incomprehensible.

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:08.340
By what possible moral calculus does U.S.
intervention to liberate 25 million people

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:13.810
forfeit moral legitimacy because it lacks
the blessing of the butchers of Tiananmen

00:38:13.810 --> 00:38:33.170
Square or the cynics of the Quai d'Orsay?

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:36.590
Which is why it is hard to take these arguments
at face value.

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:43.160
Look, we know why liberal internationalists
demanded UN sanction for the war in Iraq.

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:46.280
It was a way to stop the war.

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:49.280
It was the Gulliver effect.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:55.091
Call a committee meeting of countries with
hostile or contrary interests, i.e., the Security

00:38:55.091 --> 00:39:01.600
Council, and you have guaranteed yourself
another 12 years of inaction.

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:07.780
Historically, multilateralism is a way for
weak countries to multiply their power by

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:10.820
attaching themselves to stronger ones.

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:17.710
But multilateralism imposed on great powers,
particularly on a unipolar power, is intended

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:24.010
to restrain that power, which is precisely
why France is an ardent multilateralist.

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:26.000
But why should America be?

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:34.820
Why, in the end, does liberal internationalism
want to tie down Gulliver, to blunt the pursuit

00:39:34.820 --> 00:39:40.920
of American national interests by making it
subordinate to a myriad of other interests?

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:46.130
In the immediate post-Vietnam era, this aversion
to national interest might have been attributed

00:39:46.130 --> 00:39:48.080
to self-doubt and self-loathing.

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:49.920
I don't know.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:55.550
I leave that question to a trained psychiatrist.

00:39:55.550 --> 00:40:00.800
What I do know is that today it is a mistake
to see liberal foreign policy as deriving

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:06.590
from anti-Americanism or lack of patriotism
or a late efflorescence of '60s radicalism.

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:13.210
On the contrary, the liberal aversion to national
interest stems from an idealism, a larger

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:20.650
vision of country, a vision of some ambition
and nobility, the vision of a true international

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:21.740
community.

00:40:21.740 --> 00:40:26.860
And that is, to transmute the international
system from the Hobbesian universe into a

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:32.820
Lockean universe, to turn the state of nature
into a norm-driven community, to turn the

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:37.320
law of the jungle into the rule of law of
treaties and contracts and UN resolutions.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:44.790
In short, to remake the international system
in the image of domestic society.

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:50.140
And to create such a true international community,
you have to temper, transcend, and, in the

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:55.680
end, abolish the very idea of state power
and national interest.

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:58.990
Hence the antipathy to American hegemony and
American power.

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:04.401
If you are going to break the international
arena to the mold of domestic society, you

00:41:04.401 --> 00:41:08.930
have to domesticate its single most powerful
actor.

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:15.281
You have to abolish American dominance, not
only as an affront to fairness, but also as

00:41:15.281 --> 00:41:21.050
the greatest obstacle on the whole planet
to a democratized international system where

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:28.730
all live under self-governing international
institutions and self-reenforcing international

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:31.470
norms.

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:37.950
This vision is all very nice, all very noble,
and all very crazy.

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:43.160
And it brings us to the third great foreign
policy school, realism.

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:48.780
The realist looks at this great liberal project
and sees a hopeless illusion.

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:54.080
Because turning the Hobbesian world that has
existed since long before the Peloponnesian

00:41:54.080 --> 00:42:00.990
Wars into a Lockean world, turning a jungle
into a subdivision of the suburb, requires

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:03.650
a revolution in human nature.

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:09.110
Not just an set of new institutions, but a
revolution in human nature.

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:14.550
And realists do not believe in revolutions
of human nature, much less stake their future,

00:42:14.550 --> 00:42:17.950
and the future of their countries upon them.

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:22.960
Realism recognizes the fundamental fallacy
in the whole idea of the international system

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:25.990
being modeled on domestic society.

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:32.430
First, what holds domestic society together
is a supreme central authority wielding a

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:35.600
monopoly of power and enforcing norms.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:39.770
In the international arena, there is no such
thing.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:44.350
Domestic society may look like a place of
self-regulating norms, but if somebody breaks

00:42:44.350 --> 00:42:50.680
into your house, you call 911, and the police
arrive with guns drawn.

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.160
That's not exactly self-enforcement.

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:55.240
That's law enforcement.

00:42:55.240 --> 00:43:00.780
Second, domestic society rests on the shared
goodwill, civility and common values of its

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:02.810
individual members.

00:43:02.810 --> 00:43:10.260
What values are shared by, say, Britain, Cuba,
Yemen and Zimbabwe-all nominal members of

00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:14.020
this fiction we call the "international community?"

00:43:14.020 --> 00:43:19.930
Of course, you can have smaller communities
of shared interests-NAFTA, ANZUS, the European

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:20.970
Union.

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:26.890
But the European conceit that relations with
all nations, regardless of ideology, regardless

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:34.420
of culture, regardless even of open hostility,
should be transacted on the EU model of suasion,

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:41.330
and norms, and negotiations, and solemn contractual
agreements is an illusion.

00:43:41.330 --> 00:43:45.150
A fisheries treaty with Canada is something
real.

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:51.590
An agreed framework on plutonium processing
with the likes of North Korea is not worth

00:43:51.590 --> 00:44:01.470
the paper it is written on.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:07.540
The realist believes in the definition of
peace offered in "The Devil's Dictionary"

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:11.920
by Ambrose Bierce, "Peace, noun.

00:44:11.920 --> 00:44:16.560
In international affairs, a period of cheating
between two periods of fighting."

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:24.530
Hence, the realist axiom, the international
community is a fiction.

00:44:24.530 --> 00:44:32.360
It is not a community, it is a cacophony of
straining ambitions, disparate values and

00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:34.670
contending power.

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:38.030
What does hold the international system together?

00:44:38.030 --> 00:44:42.330
What keeps it from degenerating into total
anarchy?

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:49.220
Not the phony security of treaties, not the
best of goodwill among the nicer nations.

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:55.961
In the unipolar world we inhabit, what stability
we do enjoy today is owed to the overwhelming

00:44:55.961 --> 00:45:07.160
power and deterrent threat of the United States.

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:10.500
If someone invades your house, you call the
cops.

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:13.310
Who do you dial if someone invades your country?

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:15.430
You dial Washington.

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:20.940
In the unipolar world, the closest thing to
a centralized authority, to an enforcer of

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:24.520
norms, is America, American power.

00:45:24.520 --> 00:45:32.190
And ironically, American power is precisely
what liberal internationalism wants to constrain

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:39.480
and tie down and subsume in pursuit of some
brave new Lockean world.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:41.810
Realists don't live just in America.

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:45.340
I found one in Finland.

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:51.580
During the 1997 negotiations in Oslo over
the land mine treaty, one of the rare holdouts,

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:53.730
interestingly enough, was Finland.

00:45:53.730 --> 00:45:58.320
The Finnish Prime Minister stoutly opposed
the land mine ban.

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:02.090
And for that he was scolded by his Scandinavian
neighbors.

00:46:02.090 --> 00:46:06.720
To which he responded tartly that this was
a very convenient pose for the other Nordic

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:15.990
countries, after all, Finland is their land
mine.

00:46:15.990 --> 00:46:18.130
That one usually takes about five seconds.

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:21.730
It helps to have a map.

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:25.160
Finland is the land mine between Russia and
Scandinavia.

00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:40.690
America is the land mine between barbarism
and civilization.

00:46:40.690 --> 00:46:46.910
Where would South Korea be without America
and its land mines along the DMZ?

00:46:46.910 --> 00:46:51.970
Where would Europe, with its cozy arrogant
community be without America having saved

00:46:51.970 --> 00:47:00.460
it from the Soviet colossus?

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:06.360
Where would the Middle East be had American
power not stopped the Saddam in 1991?

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:12.520
The land mine that protects civilization from
barbarism is not parchment but power, and

00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:20.120
in a unipolar world, American power, wielded,
if necessary, unilaterally, and if necessary,

00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:21.140
preemptively.

00:47:21.140 --> 00:47:28.570
Now, those uneasy with American power have
made these two means of wielding it, preemption

00:47:28.570 --> 00:47:33.510
and unilateralism, the focus of unrelenting
criticism.

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:39.510
The doctrine of preemption, in particular,
has been widely attacked for violating international

00:47:39.510 --> 00:47:41.360
norms.

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:43.380
What international norm?

00:47:43.380 --> 00:47:50.550
The one under which in 1981, Israel was universally
condemned, even the Reagan administration

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:57.490
joined in the condemnation at the Security
Council, for preemptively destroying Osirak

00:47:57.490 --> 00:47:58.710
reactor.

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:12.180
Does anyone today doubt that it was the right
thing to do, both strategically and morally?

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:17.510
In a world of terrorists, terrorist states
and weapons of mass destruction, the option

00:48:17.510 --> 00:48:20.940
of preemption is especially necessary.

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:27.121
In the bipolar world of the Cold War, with
a stable non-suicidal adversary, deterrence

00:48:27.121 --> 00:48:29.060
could work.

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:33.070
Deterrence does not work against people who
ache for heaven.

00:48:33.070 --> 00:48:36.010
It does not work against undeterrables.

00:48:36.010 --> 00:48:40.400
And it does not work against undetectables.

00:48:40.400 --> 00:48:45.260
Non-suicidal enemy regimes that might attack
through clandestine means, a suitcase nuke

00:48:45.260 --> 00:48:48.900
or anonymously delivered anthrax.

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:56.410
Against both undeterrables and undetectables,
preemption is the only possible strategy.

00:48:56.410 --> 00:49:01.430
If anything, the doctrine of preemption against
openly hostile states pursuing weapons of

00:49:01.430 --> 00:49:05.920
mass destruction is an improvement on classical
deterrence.

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:12.620
Traditionally, we deterred the use of weapons
of mass destruction by the threat of retaliation

00:49:12.620 --> 00:49:16.640
after we'd been attacked, and that's too late.

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:21.920
The point of preemption is to deter the very
acquisition of weapons of mass destruction

00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:23.980
in the first place.

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:28.920
Whether or not Iraq had large stockpiles of
weapons of mass destruction, the very fact

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:34.980
that the United States overthrew a hostile
regime that had repeatedly refused to come

00:49:34.980 --> 00:49:39.830
clean on its weapons has had precisely this
deterrent effect.

00:49:39.830 --> 00:49:45.830
We are safer today not just because Saddam
is gone, but because Libya and Iran and others

00:49:45.830 --> 00:49:52.061
contemplating, tracking with Weapons of Mass
Destruction, have, for the first time, seen

00:49:52.061 --> 00:50:00.460
that it carries a cost, a very high cost.

00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:10.680
Yes, of course, imperfect intelligence makes
preemption problematic.

00:50:10.680 --> 00:50:14.620
But that is not an objection on principle,
it is an objection in practice.

00:50:14.620 --> 00:50:17.750
Indeed, the objection concedes the principle.

00:50:17.750 --> 00:50:20.240
We need good intelligence.

00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:26.410
But we remain defenseless if we abjure the
option of preemption.

00:50:26.410 --> 00:50:32.420
The other great objection to the way American
unipolar power has been wielded is its unilateralism.

00:50:32.420 --> 00:50:41.070
I would dispute how unilateralist we have
been, not nearly enough for my taste, but

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:42.540
no matter.

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:47.000
Look, of course, one acts in concert with
others if possible.

00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:51.070
It is nice when others join us in the breach.

00:50:51.070 --> 00:50:54.170
No one seeks to be unilateral.

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:59.040
Unilateralism simply means that one does not
allow oneself to be held hostage to the will

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:06.980
of others.

00:51:06.980 --> 00:51:12.810
Irving Kristol once explained that he preferred
the Organization of American States to the

00:51:12.810 --> 00:51:14.440
UN.

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:21.280
In the OAS, you see, we can be voted down
in only three languages, thereby saving interpreters'

00:51:21.280 --> 00:51:24.680
fees.

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:28.000
Of course, you build coalitions when possible.

00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:32.920
We garnered a coalition of the willing for
Iraq which included substantial allies like

00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:37.470
Britain, Australia, Spain, Italy and much
of Eastern Europe.

00:51:37.470 --> 00:51:42.900
France and Germany made clear from the beginning
that they would never join in the overthrow

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:44.710
of Saddam.

00:51:44.710 --> 00:51:50.600
Therefore the choice was not a wide coalition
versus a narrow one, but a narrow coalition

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:52.050
versus none.

00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:58.160
There were serious arguments against the war
in Iraq, but the fact that France did not

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:09.730
approve was not one of them.

00:52:09.730 --> 00:52:12.540
Realists choose not to be Gulliver.

00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:19.080
In an international system with no sovereign,
no police, no protection, where power is the

00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:24.850
ultimate arbiter and history has bequeathed
us unprecedented power, we should preserve

00:52:24.850 --> 00:52:30.410
that power and our freedom of action to use
it.

00:52:30.410 --> 00:52:33.780
But here we come up against the limits of
realism.

00:52:33.780 --> 00:52:37.250
You cannot live by power alone.

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:44.820
Realism is an antidote to the woolly internationalism
of the 90s, but realism can only take you

00:52:44.820 --> 00:52:46.980
so far.

00:52:46.980 --> 00:52:52.710
Its basic problem lies in its definition of
national interest as classically offered by

00:52:52.710 --> 00:52:59.700
its great theorist, Hans Morgenthau, interest
defined as power.

00:52:59.700 --> 00:53:05.541
Morgenthau postulated that what drives nations,
what drives the foreign policy, is the will

00:53:05.541 --> 00:53:09.270
to power, to keep it and expand it.

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:14.920
For most Americans, will to power might be
a correct description of the world of what

00:53:14.920 --> 00:53:20.750
motivates other countries, but it cannot be
a prescription for America.

00:53:20.750 --> 00:53:23.360
It cannot be our purpose.

00:53:23.360 --> 00:53:27.430
America cannot and will not live by realpolitik
alone.

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:31.590
Our foreign policy must be driven by something
beyond power.

00:53:31.590 --> 00:53:38.550
And unless conservatives present ideals to
challenge the liberal ideal of an international

00:53:38.550 --> 00:53:49.780
community, they will lose the debate, which
is why among American conservatives, another

00:53:49.780 --> 00:53:55.490
more idealistic school has arisen that sees
America's national interest as an expression

00:53:55.490 --> 00:53:57.360
of its values.

00:53:57.360 --> 00:54:05.310
It is this fourth school that has guided foreign
policy in this decade.

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:12.190
This conservative alternative to realism is
often lazily and invidiously called neoconservatism,

00:54:12.190 --> 00:54:19.860
but that is a very odd name for a school whose
major proponents in the world today are George

00:54:19.860 --> 00:54:23.020
W. Bush and Tony Blair.

00:54:23.020 --> 00:54:27.690
If they are neoconservatives, then I'm a liberal.

00:54:27.690 --> 00:54:31.930
There's nothing neo about Bush, and there's
nothing con about Blair.

00:54:31.930 --> 00:54:38.490
Yet they are the principal proponents today
of what might be called democratic globalism,

00:54:38.490 --> 00:54:45.850
a foreign policy that defines the national
interest not as power but as values, and that

00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:53.480
identifies one supreme value, what John Kennedy
called "the success of liberty."

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:59.480
As President Bush put it in his speech at
Whitehall last November, "The United States

00:54:59.480 --> 00:55:03.890
and Great Britain share a mission in the world
beyond the balance of power or the simple

00:55:03.890 --> 00:55:05.810
pursuit of interest.

00:55:05.810 --> 00:55:11.130
We seek the advance of freedom and the peace
that freedom brings."

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:16.950
Beyond power, beyond interest, beyond interest
defined as power.

00:55:16.950 --> 00:55:22.910
This is the credo of democratic globalism,
which explains its political appeal.

00:55:22.910 --> 00:55:30.850
America is a nation uniquely built not on
blood, or race, but on a proposition, to which

00:55:30.850 --> 00:55:36.050
its sacred honor has been pledged for 200
years.

00:55:36.050 --> 00:55:41.480
This American exceptionalism explains why
non-Americans find this foreign policy so

00:55:41.480 --> 00:55:48.040
difficult to credit, why Blair has had more
difficulty garnering support for it in his

00:55:48.040 --> 00:55:56.180
country, and why Europe, in particular, finds
this kind of value-driven foreign policy hopelessly

00:55:56.180 --> 00:55:59.930
and irritatingly moralistic.

00:55:59.930 --> 00:56:04.451
Democratic globalism sees as the engine of
history not the will to power but the will

00:56:04.451 --> 00:56:06.250
to freedom.

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:12.250
And while it has been attacked as a dreamy,
idealistic innovation, its inspiration comes

00:56:12.250 --> 00:56:20.150
from the Truman Doctrine of 1947, the Kennedy
inaugural of 1960, and Reagan's evil empire

00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:22.850
speech of 1983.

00:56:22.850 --> 00:56:29.810
They all sought to recast a struggle for power
between two geopolitical titans into a struggle

00:56:29.810 --> 00:56:35.110
between freedom and unfreedom, and, yes, good
and evil.

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:41.870
Which is why the Truman Doctrine was heavily
attacked by realists like Hans Morgenthau

00:56:41.870 --> 00:56:48.560
and George Kennan, and Reagan attacked by
the entire foreign policy establishment for

00:56:48.560 --> 00:56:55.780
the sin of ideologizing the Cold War by injecting
a moral overlay.

00:56:55.780 --> 00:56:57.490
That was then.

00:56:57.490 --> 00:57:04.990
Today, post-9/11, we find ourselves in a similar
existential struggle but with a different

00:57:04.990 --> 00:57:13.530
enemy, not Soviet communism, but Arab-Islamic
totalitarianism, both secular and religious.

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:19.550
Bush and Blair are similarly attacked for
naively and crudely casting this struggle

00:57:19.550 --> 00:57:24.560
as one of freedom versus unfreedom, good versus
evil.

00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:32.220
Now, given the way not just freedom but human
decency were suppressed in both Afghanistan

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:40.820
and Iraq, the two major wars of this new war,
you would have to give Bush and Blair's moral

00:57:40.820 --> 00:57:45.480
claims the decided advantage of being obviously
true.

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:52.620
Nonetheless, something can be true and still
be dangerous.

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:56.430
Many people are deeply uneasy with the Bush-Blair
doctrine.

00:57:56.430 --> 00:58:00.810
I dare say, many conservatives in particular.

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:06.990
When Blair declares in his address to Congress,
"The spread of freedom is our last line of

00:58:06.990 --> 00:58:13.370
defense and our first line of attack," they
see a dangerously expansive, aggressively

00:58:13.370 --> 00:58:16.790
utopian foreign policy.

00:58:16.790 --> 00:58:20.000
In short, they see Woodrow Wilson.

00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:25.810
Now, to a conservative, Woodrow Wilson is
fighting words.

00:58:25.810 --> 00:58:31.180
Yes, this vision is expansive and perhaps
utopian.

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:33.240
But it ain't Wilsonian.

00:58:33.240 --> 00:58:38.760
Wilson envisioned the spread of democratic
values through as-yet-to-be invented international

00:58:38.760 --> 00:58:40.670
institutions.

00:58:40.670 --> 00:58:43.130
He could be forgiven for that.

00:58:43.130 --> 00:58:50.520
In 1918, there was no way to know how utterly
corrupt and useless these international institutions

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:55.940
would turn out to be.

00:58:55.940 --> 00:59:06.840
Eight decades of bitter experience later,
with Libya chairing the UN Commission on Human

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:11.240
Rights, there is no way not to know.

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:14.500
Democratic globalism is not Wilsonian.

00:59:14.500 --> 00:59:19.770
Its attractiveness is precisely that it shares
realism's insights about the centrality of

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:21.020
power.

00:59:21.020 --> 00:59:26.590
Its attractiveness is precisely that it has
appropriate contempt for the fictional legalisms

00:59:26.590 --> 00:59:29.220
of liberal internationalism.

00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:34.630
Moreover, democratic globalism is an improvement
over realism.

00:59:34.630 --> 00:59:39.990
What it can teach realism is that the spread
of democracy is not just an end but a means,

00:59:39.990 --> 00:59:44.230
an indispensable means for securing American
interests.

00:59:44.230 --> 00:59:46.420
The reason is simple.

00:59:46.420 --> 00:59:51.840
Democracies are inherently more friendly to
the United States, less belligerent to their

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:55.860
neighbors, and more inclined to peace.

00:59:55.860 --> 01:00:00.630
Realists are right that to protect your interests
you often have to go around the world bashing

01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:03.420
bad guys over the head.

01:00:03.420 --> 01:00:09.020
But that technique, no matter how satisfying,
has its limits.

01:00:09.020 --> 01:00:13.920
At some point, you have to implant something,
something organic.

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:16.430
And that something is democracy.

01:00:16.430 --> 01:00:18.410
But where?

01:00:18.410 --> 01:00:23.990
The danger of democratic globalism is its
universalism, its open-ended commitment to

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:30.490
human freedom, its temptation to plant the
flag of democracy everywhere.

01:00:30.490 --> 01:00:33.140
It must learn to say no.

01:00:33.140 --> 01:00:36.370
And indeed, it does say no.

01:00:36.370 --> 01:00:43.860
But when it says no to Liberia, or Congo,
or Burma, or countenances alliances with authoritarian

01:00:43.860 --> 01:00:52.190
rulers in places like Pakistan and, for that
matter, Russia, it stands accused of hypocrisy,

01:00:52.190 --> 01:00:56.550
which is why we must articulate criteria for
saying yes.

01:00:56.550 --> 01:01:00.200
And I propose a single criteria, where to
intervene?

01:01:00.200 --> 01:01:02.100
Where to bring democracy?

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:04.460
Where to nation-build?

01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:06.390
Where it counts?

01:01:06.390 --> 01:01:08.420
Call it democratic realism.

01:01:08.420 --> 01:01:14.750
And this is its axiom: We will support democracy
everywhere, but we will commit blood and treasure

01:01:14.750 --> 01:01:22.410
in places where there is a strategic necessity,
meaning, a place central to the larger war

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:29.240
against the existential enemy, the enemy that
poses a global mortal threat to freedom.

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:31.770
Where does it count?

01:01:31.770 --> 01:01:35.130
Fifty years ago, Germany and Japan counted.

01:01:35.130 --> 01:01:36.340
Why?

01:01:36.340 --> 01:01:42.050
Because they were the seeds of the greatest
global threat to freedom in mid-century, fascism,

01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:51.260
and then were turned into bulwarks against
the new, next great threat, the Soviet Union

01:01:51.260 --> 01:01:54.440
and Soviet communism.

01:01:54.440 --> 01:01:56.930
Where does it count today?

01:01:56.930 --> 01:02:02.390
Where the overthrow of radicalism and the
beginnings of democracy can have a decisive

01:02:02.390 --> 01:02:09.690
effect in the war against the new global threat
to freedom, the new existential enemy, the

01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:16.820
Arab-Islamic totalitarianism that has threatened
us both in its secular and religious forms

01:02:16.820 --> 01:02:22.910
for the quarter-century since the Khomeini
revolution of 1979.

01:02:22.910 --> 01:02:29.220
Establishing civilized, decent, non-belligerent,
pro-Western polities in Afghanistan and Iraq

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:35.040
and ultimately their key neighbors would,
like the flipping of Germany and Japan in

01:02:35.040 --> 01:02:41.680
the 40s, change the strategic balance in the
fight against Arab-Islamic radicalism.

01:02:41.680 --> 01:02:46.200
Yes, it may be a bridge too far.

01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:51.840
Realists have been warning against the hubris
of thinking we can transmute an alien culture

01:02:51.840 --> 01:02:58.630
because of some postulated natural and universal
human will to freedom.

01:02:58.630 --> 01:03:03.240
And they may be right, but how do they know
in advance?

01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:08.400
Half a century ago, we heard the same confident
warnings about the imperviousness to democracy

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:11.010
of Confucian culture.

01:03:11.010 --> 01:03:13.650
That proved stunningly wrong.

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:17.710
Where is it written that Arabs are incapable
of democracy?

01:03:17.710 --> 01:03:25.292
Yes, the undertaking as in Germany and Japan
is enormous, ambitious, arrogant, and it may

01:03:25.292 --> 01:03:27.310
not succeed.

01:03:27.310 --> 01:03:30.450
But we cannot afford not to try.

01:03:30.450 --> 01:03:35.630
There is not a single, remotely plausible,
alternative strategy for attacking the monster

01:03:35.630 --> 01:03:37.990
behind 9/11.

01:03:37.990 --> 01:03:44.300
It was not Osama bin Laden, it's the cauldron
of political oppression, religious intolerance,

01:03:44.300 --> 01:03:50.960
and social ruin in the Arab-Islamic ,oppression
transmuted and deflected by regimes with no

01:03:50.960 --> 01:03:56.540
legitimacy into murderous anti-Americanism.

01:03:56.540 --> 01:03:58.430
It's not one man, it is a condition.

01:03:58.430 --> 01:04:05.050
It will be nice to find that man and to hang
him, but that's the cops-and-robbers law-enforcement

01:04:05.050 --> 01:04:12.810
model of fighting terror that we tried for
20 years and that gave us 9/11.

01:04:12.810 --> 01:04:18.619
This is war, and in war, arresting murderers
is nice.

01:04:18.619 --> 01:04:32.840
But you win by taking territory and leaving
something behind.

01:04:32.840 --> 01:04:36.990
We are the unipolar power, and what do we
do?

01:04:36.990 --> 01:04:43.220
In August, 1900, David Hilbert gave a speech
to the International Congress of Mathematicians

01:04:43.220 --> 01:04:51.610
naming 20 still unsolved mathematical problems
bequeathed by the 19th Century to the 20th.

01:04:51.610 --> 01:04:57.520
Only three remained by the way, but the soup
is getting cold, so that's for another night.

01:04:57.520 --> 01:05:03.540
Had he presented the great unsolved geopolitical
problems bequeathed to the 20th century, one

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:08.090
would have stood out above all, the rise of
Germany and its accommodation within the European

01:05:08.090 --> 01:05:10.190
state system.

01:05:10.190 --> 01:05:17.170
Similarly today, at the dawn of the 21st Century,
we can see clearly the two great geopolitical

01:05:17.170 --> 01:05:24.040
challenges on the horizon, the inexorable
rise of China and the coming demographic collapse

01:05:24.040 --> 01:05:30.310
of Europe, both of which will irrevocably
alter the equilibrium of the international

01:05:30.310 --> 01:05:32.000
system.

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:33.980
But these problems come later.

01:05:33.980 --> 01:05:36.480
They are for mid-century.

01:05:36.480 --> 01:05:39.020
They are for the young people here tonight.

01:05:39.020 --> 01:05:46.380
And we won't even get to these problems unless
we first deal with our problem.

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:52.470
And our problem is 9/11 and the roots of Arab-Islamic
nihilism.

01:05:52.470 --> 01:05:58.450
Nine-eleven felt like a new problem, but for
all its shock and surprise, it is an old problem

01:05:58.450 --> 01:06:00.160
with a new face.

01:06:00.160 --> 01:06:06.650
Nine-eleven felt like the initiation of a
new history, but it was a return to history,

01:06:06.650 --> 01:06:10.680
the history of radical ideologies and existential
enemies.

01:06:10.680 --> 01:06:13.290
The anomaly is not the world of today.

01:06:13.290 --> 01:06:16.420
The anomaly was the '90s, our holiday from
history.

01:06:16.420 --> 01:06:29.220
It felt like peace, but it was an interval
of dreaming between two periods of reality.

01:06:29.220 --> 01:06:31.460
Nine-eleven woke us up.

01:06:31.460 --> 01:06:34.910
It startled us into thinking that everything
was new.

01:06:34.910 --> 01:06:36.650
It's not.

01:06:36.650 --> 01:06:42.300
What is new is what happened not on 9/11 but
10 years earlier, on December 26, 1991, the

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:49.090
emergence of the United States as the world's
unipolar power.

01:06:49.090 --> 01:06:54.020
What is unique is our advantage in this new
struggle, an advantage we did not have during

01:06:54.020 --> 01:06:56.010
the struggles of the 20th Century.

01:06:56.010 --> 01:07:01.310
The question for our time is how to press
this advantage, how to exploit our unipolar

01:07:01.310 --> 01:07:09.500
power, how to deploy it to win the old/new
war that exploded upon us on 9/11.

01:07:09.500 --> 01:07:12.350
What is the unipolar power to do?

01:07:12.350 --> 01:07:16.300
Four schools, four answers.

01:07:16.300 --> 01:07:23.410
The isolationists want simply to ignore unipolarity,
pull up the drawbridge, and defend Fortress

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:24.410
America.

01:07:24.410 --> 01:07:30.800
Alas, the fortress has no moat, not after
the airplane, the submarine, the ballistic

01:07:30.800 --> 01:07:37.480
missile, and as for the drawbridge, it was
blown up on 9/11.

01:07:37.480 --> 01:07:39.340
Then there are the liberal internationalists.

01:07:39.340 --> 01:07:44.790
They like to dream, and to the extent they
are aware of our unipolar power, they don't

01:07:44.790 --> 01:07:46.110
like it.

01:07:46.110 --> 01:07:52.990
They see its use for anything other than humanitarianism
or reflexive self-defense as an expression

01:07:52.990 --> 01:07:55.820
of national selfishness.

01:07:55.820 --> 01:08:00.170
And they don't just want us to ignore our
unique power, they want us to yield it piece

01:08:00.170 --> 01:08:06.220
by piece, by subsuming ourselves in a new
global architecture in which America becomes

01:08:06.220 --> 01:08:12.900
not the arbiter of international events, but
a good and tame international citizen.

01:08:12.900 --> 01:08:18.270
And then there is realism, which has the clearest
understanding of the new unipolarity and its

01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:22.500
uses, unilateral and preemptive if necessary.

01:08:22.500 --> 01:08:26.710
But in the end, realism fails, because it
offers no vision.

01:08:26.710 --> 01:08:30.359
It is all means and no ends.

01:08:30.359 --> 01:08:33.570
It cannot adequately define our mission.

01:08:33.570 --> 01:08:38.230
Hence, the fourth school, democratic globalism.

01:08:38.230 --> 01:08:46.380
It has, in this decade, rallied the American
people to a struggle over values.

01:08:46.380 --> 01:08:51.359
It seeks to vindicate the American idea by
making the spread of democracy, the success

01:08:51.359 --> 01:08:56.339
of liberty, the ends and means of American
foreign policy.

01:08:56.339 --> 01:08:58.250
I support that.

01:08:58.250 --> 01:08:59.900
I applaud that.

01:08:59.900 --> 01:09:08.859
But I believe it must be tempered in its universalistic
aspirations and rhetoric from a democratic

01:09:08.859 --> 01:09:12.920
globalism to a democratic realism.

01:09:12.920 --> 01:09:17.380
It must be targeted, focused and limited.

01:09:17.380 --> 01:09:22.549
We are the friends of all, but we come ashore
only where it really counts.

01:09:22.549 --> 01:09:29.750
And where it counts today is the Islamic crescent
stretching from North Africa to Afghanistan.

01:09:29.750 --> 01:09:35.950
My friends, in October 1962, for 13 days,
we came to the edge of the abyss.

01:09:35.950 --> 01:09:43.089
Then, accompanied by our equally shaken adversary,
we drew back.

01:09:43.089 --> 01:09:50.540
On September 11, 2001, we saw the face of
Armageddon again, but this time with an enemy

01:09:50.540 --> 01:09:53.400
that does not draw back.

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:56.410
This time with an enemy that knows no reason.

01:09:56.410 --> 01:10:02.770
Were that the only difference between now
and then, our situation would be hopeless.

01:10:02.770 --> 01:10:10.390
But there is a second difference between now
and then, the uniqueness of our power, unrivaled,

01:10:10.390 --> 01:10:15.840
not just today but ever, and that evens the
odds.

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:21.190
The rationality of the enemy is something
beyond our control, but the use of our power

01:10:21.190 --> 01:10:23.440
is within our control.

01:10:23.440 --> 01:10:31.670
And if that power is used, constrained not
by illusions and fictions, but only by the

01:10:31.670 --> 01:10:40.200
limits of our mission, which is to bring a
modicum of freedom as an antidote to nihilism,

01:10:40.200 --> 01:10:42.760
we can and we will prevail.

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:43.350
Thank you, very much.

